From 3d3d2e1d1e3464aae4abadfd85f78125e1ca6d61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: autoresearch <agent@localhost>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 04:17:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] diar: run_layer0_isolated - a genuinely sound oracle for the
 one-runtime port

docs/one-runtime-merge.md SS12 already showed ggml_set_output does not protect a per-layer stage from a
LATER, structurally identical layer's tensor landing on the same gallocr slot within the running 32-layer
graph (02_qkv, 05_v, 07_oproj, 11_ffn_out all read back corrupted despite the flag). This adds
run_layer0_isolated(): the same real weights and module code (build_encoder_layer, unchanged), rebuilt as its
own graph with nothing else in it, called opt-in from Session::encode() behind AUDIOCPP_LAYER0_ISOLATED
(session.cpp), using the same input/valid_frames already captured for AUDIOCPP_DUMP_LAYER0.

Verified on device: the isolated harness's final output is byte-identical to the production graph's real
layer0_out for every window tried, and 7 of 12 traced stages (01_norm1, 03_q, 04_k, 06_attn_raw, 08_resid1,
10_gelu, 12_resid2) now match the production trace exactly, up from 0 previously trustworthy stages.

Getting there took two of this function's own bugs, both real and both instructive:
1. First draft called ggml_backend_buffer_free() (not merely ggml_gallocr_free) right after compute, before
   reading results - reading after that point silently returns zeros with no error, which produced a
   maximally confusing "everything computes to zero, status=SUCCESS" symptom that survived a dozen
   independent bisections (fresh vs shared backend, thread count, arena size, gallocr vs alloc_ctx_tensors,
   graph size 16 through 1024) before a minimal side-by-side reproduction (RAWTEST5, same context, same
   op, freeing after vs before the read) isolated it to exactly this.
2. Second bug, found immediately after fixing the first: the STAGE tensors are read back by the CALLER
   (session.cpp) after run_layer0_isolated returns, so freeing the allocator inside the function - even
   after reading `output` - left the stage tensors' data freed by the time the caller got to them. Fixed by
   giving Layer0IsolatedResult ownership of the allocator (encoder.h) and freeing it only in the destructor,
   once the caller is done.

Also newly found even in true isolation: 07_oproj and 11_ffn_out alias EACH OTHER (identical bytes) despite
both being ggml_set_output - a narrower version of the SS12 problem that persists even with no other layer
in the graph. Not yet understood; documented in encoder.h and left as the next question rather than papered
over, since the final output and 7 other stages are enough to make real progress on the port comparison.

